-- title:
--   The origin is a lattice point
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathbb{Z}^d \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ denote the integer lattice `lattice d`, the set of points
--   of `EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)` all of whose coordinates are integers. Then
--
--   $$0 \;\in\; \mathbb{Z}^d .$$
--
--   Trivial as it is, this is the lemma that supplies a base point for the discrete constructions: the
--   lattice is non-empty, so arguments that pick a lattice point of minimal distance to a given target,
--   or that start a chain of jumps at a lattice site, always have somewhere to begin.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The dimension $d$ is implicit and unconstrained; for $d = 0$ the space is a
--   single point and the statement is still true.
